# Connectivity and Setup (General Coding)

Apply these whenever code creates or depends on **multiple related pieces** (UI tab + panel, service + UI, resource + handle, etc.):

## General principle

**Don’t assume “one part exists” means “the whole feature works.”**  
Check that **all required pieces are present and connected**. If one is missing, create or wire it; don’t early-return in a way that leaves the feature half-set up and impossible to fix on a later run.

## Lesson from Paint tab not appearing

- **Symptom**: Paint panel existed but no tab to open it → feature invisible to the user.
- **Cause**: Early return when `_Paint_Panel != null` meant the **tab was never created** when the panel was already assigned.
- **Takeaway**: The tab is the only way to open the panel (connectivity). Skipping work because “panel exists” broke that link.

Use the same mindset elsewhere: whenever you have A and B that must both exist for a feature to work, avoid “if A exists then return” unless you also verify B exists.

## Rules

1. **Check connectivity, not just one ref.**  
   For “tab + panel”, “client + server”, “data + view”, etc.: only skip setup when **all** required pieces are present. If you skip because one ref is set, the others may never be created.

2. **Resolve refs before deciding to skip.**  
   Resolve dependencies (e.g. tab strip, parent, service) **before** any “already set up” early return. Otherwise a transient null can cause an early exit and leave state inconsistent; the next run may still see “one part set” and never retry.

3. **Avoid half-set-up state.**  
   Don’t assign or persist one part (e.g. panel ref) until the other part (e.g. tab) is successfully created. Otherwise you can end up with “panel with no tab” or “handle with no resource” and no code path that fixes it.

4. **Allow retries.**  
   If creation can fail partway (e.g. tab strip null), don’t set the “success” ref so that the next call thinks everything is done. Clear or avoid assigning until the full setup succeeds, so the next run can retry.

5. **Use existence checks for “the other half”.**  
   When adding a second piece (e.g. a tab) that opens or uses the first (e.g. a panel), check “does the second piece already exist?” (e.g. `HasTab("id")`) and create it only when missing, so you don’t duplicate and you fix “panel exists, tab missing.”

## Where this applies

- Runtime UI: tab + panel, ribbon + content, menu + handler.
- Services / singletons: instance + registration or subscription.
- Resources: alloc/create + ref/handle; don’t store the ref if creation of the paired piece failed.
- Any “ensure X exists” logic that depends on Y: ensure both X and Y, and that they are connected.
